PATH C — TestFit → Dynamo/Revit
Step 1: Build a feasibility study in TestFit
Go to your TestFit account, start a new Site project.
Draw or import your site boundary.
Pick a building typology preset (wrap, podium, tower, donut, hotel) and set unit mix / parking targets.
TestFit's AI generates optimized massing and unit layouts in seconds — adjust sliders to explore variants live.
Step 2: Bring it into Revit
From TestFit, use the export option to send data to Dynamo/Revit (TestFit integrates with Dynamo as the bridge into Revit).
In Revit's Dynamo window, run the corresponding TestFit import graph/package to rebuild the massing as native Revit geometry.
Convert resulting masses to walls/floors/roofs as in Path A, Step 3.
---
Common Mistakes to Avoid
Trusting AI output as final/code-compliant. All three tools (Forma, ChatGPT node, TestFit) produce starting points — always verify against local zoning and building code yourself.
Skipping the site boundary step in Forma or TestFit. Massing generated without a defined boundary ignores your actual property line/setbacks.
Hardcoding API keys in Dynamo graphs. If you share or version-control a graph with your OpenAI key typed directly into a node, anyone with the file can use your key. Use a separate input node or environment variable instead.
Leaving masses visible in construction documents. After `Model by Face` conversion, forgetting to hide the original mass (`GM`) causes duplicate geometry in views/schedules.
Ignoring performance analysis. Picking the massing option that "looks best" without running Forma's Sun/Wind/Noise analysis defeats the purpose of using an AI+data workflow.
Running Dynamo graphs with no internet. ChatGPT and Forma nodes/extensions require a live connection — a network drop mid-run causes silent failures or timeouts.
---
Optimization Tips
Be specific in your Forma program inputs and ChatGPT prompts. Vague inputs = vague/irrelevant AI output. "4-story mixed-use, 30% ground-floor retail, urban infill" beats "a building."
Batch your Forma analyses (Sun + Wind + Noise together on 3–4 shortlisted options) rather than one at a time — it runs in parallel in the cloud.
Keep AI-imported masses on their own workset in team Revit files so they can be isolated/hidden without disturbing other disciplines.
Purge unused elements regularly (`Manage` → `Purge Unused`) after multiple AI-generated imports — each iteration can bloat file size.
Save Dynamo graphs as reusable custom nodes once a ChatGPT-assisted workflow proves useful, so you're not rebuilding the wiring every project.
Version your TestFit/Forma iterations as separate proposals, not overwritten edits, so you can compare AI-generated options side by side later.
